2 CONC >	mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman";</line><line>	mso-fareast-theme-font:minor-fareast;</line><line>	mso-hansi-font-family:Calibri;</line><line>	mso-hansi-theme-font:minor-latin;</line><line>	mso-bidi-font-family:"Times New Roman";</line><l
2 CONC ine>	mso-bidi-theme-font:minor-bidi;}</line><line>&lt;/style&gt;</line><line>&lt;![endif]--&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;span style="font-size: 16pt;" lang="EN-GB"&gt;Sutcliffes of Pendleton and areas around&lt;/span&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;s
2 CONC pan&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;span style="font-size: 16pt;" lang="EN-GB"&gt;I)Pendleton&lt;/span&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;Most research into S
2 CONC utcliffe family history concerning the area around Pendleton always returns to a single man, Isaac Sutcliffe. There are, seemingly, no clear records about his date of birth and most years published on the IGI are mere estimates. The one solid fac
2 CONC t is that an Isaac Sutcliffe of Padiham married a certain Mary Morris of Pendleton on 6 May 1749 in Whalley. This church, St Mary and All Saints, remains the focal point of Sutcliffe family history research for the next&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/s
2 CONC pan&gt;hundred years.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;Isaac&amp;rsquo;s parents are at present not known. If we accept that he really came from Padiham, then we are sorely disappointed when searching the Parish Registers o
2 CONC f St Leonard&amp;rsquo;s as there is no trace of any Isaac in these records. There are a number of Sutcliffes but none of them can be linked to this person. It is, as we shall see later with the resettlement order, possible that he actually came fr
2 CONC om more easterly parts in the area of Calderdale such as Hebden Bridge or Heptonstall, which seems to have been the origin of a number of Sutcliffes included in this register.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;It can certainl
2 CONC y be claimed that he and his wife produced a large amount of children; the first, Mary, being baptised on 17 January 1749, four months before Isaac&amp;rsquo;s marriage to Mary Morris. The rest, eight in all, were born at&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;
2 CONC /span&gt;two to five year intervals between 1751 and 1771. Two of these male children, William (1756-1821) and Isaac (1761-1843) started lines with descendants who were to spread outside the neighbouring parishes of Pendleton.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt
2 CONC ;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;An interesting document in the Lancashire Record Office QSP 1780/2 from 21 May 1759 contests the right of settlement of Isaac and his family in Pendleton. After a complaint by the &lt;em&gt;Church-Wardens and Over
2 CONC seers of the Poor of the Township of Pendleton&lt;/em&gt; two JPs support the notion that &lt;em&gt;Isaac Sutcliffe and Mary his wife and Mary Elizabeth William John and Matthew their children &amp;hellip;Poor Persons and likely to become chargeabl
2 CONC e to the Township&amp;hellip;&lt;/em&gt;have no legal right of settlement there and should be removed to the &amp;ldquo;Township of Warley in the West-Ryding of York&amp;rdquo;, the supposed place of their past legal settlement. Although the name
2 CONC s of the five children all fit those born to Isaac and Mary before 1759, it would&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;seem that this resettlement order was never executed as those born after 1759 continued to be recorded as children of Isaac of Pend
2 CONC leton, weaver. A hand-written note under the document seems to indicate this verdict: &amp;ldquo;stayed &amp;ndash; on appeal&amp;rdquo;.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;Isaac was buried in Whalley on 18 March 1786. His wife, Mary, had precede
2 CONC d him, being buried on 16 February 1786.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;William Sutcliffe (1756-1821) married Margaret &amp;lsquo;Peggy&amp;rsquo; Dawson on 3 August 1778. Ten children were the product of this marriage in t
2 CONC he years between 1779 and 1798, all of whom were baptised at Whalley and recorded as children of William Sutcliffe, (common) carrier. Seven of these were to marry and can be traced on the 1841 Census. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&l
2 CONC t;span&gt;One document in the Lancashire Record Office DDX 28/249/89 (19 October 1798)&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;appears to refer to this William: it is a claim by Mary Whittaker of&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;Whins for debts of thr
2 CONC ee pounds two shillings and six pence against William Sutcliffe in the form of unpaid rent.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;span style="font-size: 16pt;" lang="EN-GB"&gt;II
2 CONC ) Sabden&lt;/span&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;The&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;family of William supplies the first generation of Sutcliffes to settle in Sabden. At l
2 CONC east three male members were recorded as living there in 1833 by Dr Laycock in his &amp;ldquo;Population of Sabden Valley 1829-1839&amp;rdquo;. Matthew (b.1783), after his marriage to Mary Pilkington on 14 February 1803, could be found on Chalk Row
2 CONC , Sabden together with his brother, Robert, who married Jane Slaytor on 24 August 1821. Their brother, Richard ( b.1792), resided on Top Row. He had married Mary Pickard on 1 January 1820.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;Th
2 CONC e 1841 census reveals the growth of each of these families. Matthew, working as an agricultural labourer, now had five children between the ages of 32 and 4 years old in his household in Step Row. One of his older children no longer living at hom
2 CONC e was possibly the George Sutcliffe, a bleacher,&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;living in Dial Row, Sabden. He was married to an Ellen. In 1871 George, now alone , was living at 31 Top Row. He was still a bleacher.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line>
2 CONC <line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;Richard , a coal carrier, lived in Top Row and had four children under his roof. A consultation of the Whalley baptismal records between 1813 and 1837 shows that Richard and Mary, in at least three cases, delayed the bapt
2 CONC ism of some of their&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;children for a number of years after their birth. Thus, Mary Ann, born 18 May 1822, was first baptised on 17 April 1831 at Whalley. Similarly, in the cases of Margaret, born 7 February 1825, b
2 CONC aptised 22 April 1832 and Nancy, born 16 March 1828, baptised 7 April 1833. A practice which was also copied by his brother Robert, whose daughter Alice was born&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;on 4 March 1829 but baptised on 7 April 1833. I hav
2 CONC e been unable to find any male heirs for Richard to continue this line.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;Robert, like his brother Matthew, was resident in Step Row in the 1841 census. A coal man, too, he had 6 children. Thi
2 CONC s was the line of the family who were later to establish themselves as grocers and sub-postmasters. Robert born in 1838 married Mary Bond of Higham&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;in September 1860. He was a general labourer who lived in Step Ro
2 CONC w. The trade of&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;shopkeeping began with his son Robert who was born in 1863. He married Elizabeth Ann Mitchell of Wiswell in 1883 and learnt his trade as an assistant shopkeeper for Coop Stores, later taking over h
2 CONC is own grocery business and&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;becoming Sabden&amp;rsquo;s postmaster. The family tradition of naming one of the sons Robert was continued with his own son, born in 1900.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&
2 CONC lt;span&gt;Other Sutcliffes in Sabden at the time of the census were Joseph Sutcliffe (30), dyer, in Pump Row; Whittaker Sutcliffe( 14), colour maker, in Crow Tree Row and John Sutcliffe (25), block cutter, in Henry Bury Road. There were about 29 p
2 CONC eople named Sutcliffe there at that time.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;One cause of irritation and confusion in the pursuit of accurate family history is the proliferation of&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;identical f
2 CONC orenames among members of the same or different generations of a family. The&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;case with the name of &amp;ldquo;Robert&amp;rdquo; proved easy to follow because it became typical for the Sabden Sutcliffes - handed do
2 CONC wn from one generation to the next in the same line. The problems caused by incorrect genealogical alignment of family members with&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;the name of &amp;ldquo;William&amp;rdquo; or &amp;ldquo;Isaac&amp;rdquo; were mor
2 CONC e difficult to solve. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;span lang="EN-GB"&gt;III) Wiswell and Australia&lt;/span&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&l
2 CONC t;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;Isaac (1761-1843) was a son of the original Isaac of Pendleton, weaver. His brother was therefore the William (1756-1821), whose sons &amp;lsquo;migrated&amp;rsquo; to Sabden . Isaac formed his own little S
2 CONC utcliffe &amp;lsquo;dynasty&amp;rsquo; in Wiswell after marriage to Isabelle Coulthurst on 4 December 1788. In 1828 Dr. Laycock records him as being occupant of Wiswell Mill farm. A monumental Inscription in Whalley churchyard in the form of a fla
2 CONC t stone in the grass records the life spans of this family group.(plot B85).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span&gt;Sacred to the mem
2 CONC ory of Isaac Sutcliffe of Wiswell who departed this life on the 27&lt;sup&gt;th&lt;/sup&gt; day of January 1843, aged 82 years. Also Isabel the wife of Isaac Sutcliffe who departed this life on the 9&lt;sup&gt;th&lt;/sup&gt; day of August 1799, age
2 CONC d 38 years. Also of Matthew Sutcliffe of Billington their son who departed this life on the 20&lt;sup&gt;th&lt;/sup&gt; day of February 1830, aged 37 years. Also of James Sutcliffe their son who departed this life on the 3&lt;sup&gt;rd&lt;/sup&gt
2 CONC ; day of February 1831,aged 36 years. Also of Jane Sutcliffe their daughter who departed this life on the 6&lt;sup&gt;th&lt;/sup&gt; day of May 1843, aged 52 years. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/
2 CONC span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;Matthew ( 1792-1830) moved to Billington after his marriage to Esther Whitehead at St. Mary&amp;rsquo;s, Blackburn on 3 May 1814. His son, Isaac, baptised in Langho,&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/spa
2 CONC n&gt;Billington on 19 May 1816, married and moved with his wife to Pendleton, Salford where he worked as a joiner. James&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;(1795-1831) after his marriage to Mary Monk of Altham on 3 March 1816 stayed in the area aro
2 CONC und Pendleton and Wiswell. All these migrations away from the area of birth were still solidly within the perimeters of Lancashire, but it was an Isaac, who is not mentioned in the inscription, but was&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;the son o
2 CONC f Isaac Sutcliffe of Wiswell ( baptised 7 May 1797 in Whalley) who provides links to places further afield. After his marriage to Rachel Exton at Whalley on 7 December 1824, he settled in Tottington Lower End, Bury. His son, Isaac, baptised in Whal
2 CONC ley on 7 August 1825, married&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;Ellen Grimshaw of Blackrod&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;in 1847 and after a brief residence in Walmersley as a cotton jobber decided to emigrate to Australia, departing on the s
2 CONC hip &amp;ldquo;Hope&amp;rdquo; in 1852. A son, Isaac (1860-1899), born in Alma, Victoria became a school principal there. Isaac from Wiswell ,&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;sometime of Tottington Lower End, died in Clunes, Victoria in 1886.&lt
2 CONC ;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;span lang="EN-GB"&gt;IV) Billington and Mitton&lt;/span&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt
2 CONC ;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;A nephew of Isaac of Wiswell (1761-1843) and son of William of Pendleton ( 1756-1821) supplies us with yet another Sutcliffe named Isaac. He was the first born son of the aforementioned William , baptise
2 CONC d in Whalley on 25 July 1779. In the baptismal records there is the name of a possible twin brother, David, who was baptised on the same day. So far, I have been unable to find further records for this person. For Isaac (1779-1841), however, I ca
2 CONC n provide a clear and detailed line up until the present; this line includes myself, Isaac being my great -great- great -grandfather. The genealogy for this particular Isaac is complicated by the confusion and misunderstandings apparent in the entr
2 CONC ies in the IGI records. Not unsurprisingly, uncle and nephew are often mixed up and the date of birth for his wife, Alice Slinger, has only been established after the discovery of the family Bible still in the hands of the Slingers at their farm i
2 CONC n Pendleton. Isaac married Alice Slinger in Whalley on 22 September 1817; both of them were living in Pendleton at that time. Alice Slinger&amp;rsquo;s date of birth was 19 February 1785 so that, like her husband Isaac, she was in her thirties befo
2 CONC re she married. The Whalley records note a child born out of wedlock to an Alice Slinger of Pendleton named Dorothy Boothman, who was&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;baptised on 21 December 1810. Although it would seem likely that this might b
2 CONC e the same Alice, I have found nothing to verify this up to the present date. Intriguingly, both Joseph and William&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;Boothman were farmers in Pendleton at that time. In earlier years Lawrence Boothman had also bee
2 CONC n the local blacksmith so that the name must have been very familiar to the people living there. Despite this, I can find no further reference to Dorothy Boothman and she appears not to&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;have been part of Isaac&amp
2 CONC ;rsquo;s later family after 1817.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;Alice&amp;rsquo;s parents, Robert Slinger (1761-1839) and Mary Newsham (1754-1830), began farming in Pendleton in the 1780s, thus starting a chain of generational succession tha
2 CONC t remains unbroken up until the present. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;During their time in Pendleton between 1817 and 1826, Alice and Isaac had four children. Mary baptised 27 September 1818; William baptised 26 August 1
2 CONC 821; Robert, baptised 28 Sept 1823 and&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;Margaret, baptised 12 March 1826. At some time during the end of the decade they moved to Billington where their final son, James, was born. He, too. was baptised in Whalle
2 CONC y on 15 March 1829. Two documents in the Lancashire Record Office probably relate to Isaac during his time spent at Billington. PR 239/59, although not dated, is signed by William Hesmondhalgh, superintendent of the Billington Lunatic Asylum from 1
2 CONC 820 until his death in 1834. This would put the date of the note he wrote to the overseer of Billington vestry, Mr James Seed, at some time around 1830.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line
2 CONC ><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span&gt;Pay Isaac Sutcliffe for his bit of road as I find it in sufficient order.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&l
2 CONC t;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;The second PR 2391/37 is dated 7 July 1832. It is a request from a poor parishioner, R. Slaytor, for intercession on his behalf so that he may be granted relief by the same overseer mentioned in the previous document, Mr James Se
2 CONC ed.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;span lang="EN-GB"&gt;Mr Sutcliffe you must take this paper to James Seed the overseer and get me the one pound that was granted at the las
2 CONC t rent meeting and tell him he has some money to pay and he will not look of it any longer. So you must not fail in getting me it &lt;/span&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;Th
2 CONC e addressing of Isaac in such a way seems to show that he had some standing in the local community as a farmer and also social contact to the overseer, possibly as a member of the vestry commission, .&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt
2 CONC ;span&gt;The 1841 census for Mitton in the Township of Great Mitton (HO 107/1321/15 p.9) provides a further glimpse of Isaac&amp;rsquo;s family. Although the census was included under Yorkshire, there may have been a mistake made by the enumerato
2 CONC r and Mitton should have&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;actually been part of Henthorn and Coldcotes, and therefore a township in Whalley parish, Lancashire. This part of Mitton is situated at the confluence of the rivers Hodder and Ribble,&lt;
2 CONC span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;2&amp;frac34; miles SW of Clitheroe. Great Mitton and the West Riding of Yorkshire are on the other side of the &amp;lsquo;delta&amp;rsquo; accessible by a bridge. Mitton Hall lies on the&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/s
2 CONC pan&gt;Lancashire side and this part is sometimes&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;referred to as Little Mitton.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;Another obstacle to an accurate identification of this family was the practic
2 CONC e used by the enumerator of rounding up or down the ages of the family members into units of five. Thus Isaac was recorded as being 60, although he was&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;at least one year older, and his daughter, Mary, as 20 althou
2 CONC gh she was nearer 22. Isaac&amp;rsquo;s occupation was given as &amp;lsquo;farmer&amp;rsquo; so that many things seemed to fit the general biographical profile despite the fact that two important members of the family were not recorded as being res
2 CONC ident there. These were Isaac&amp;rsquo;s wife Alice, who had died in the previous year on 31 August 1840, and his son Robert who was to be found on page 12 of the same census as an apprentice blacksmith to John Whitaker near Great Mitton Hall. Cor
2 CONC roboration that Isaac and Alice resided in Mitton can be supplied by a monumental inscription still intact and legible in Whalley graveyard.( D 158)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt
2 CONC ;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span&gt;In remembrance of Isaac Sutcliffe of Mitton who died March 30&lt;sup&gt;th&lt;/sup&gt; 1842 aged 64 years also, Alice his wife who died &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span&gt;August&lt;em&gt; 31&l
2 CONC t;sup&gt;st&lt;/sup&gt; 1840 aged 56 years.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;After the death of their parents the children of Isaac and Alice all m
2 CONC oved to new areas. Mary married William Bailey of Bashall Eaves ( 1814-1886) in Great Mitton, All Hallows&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;on 24&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;April 1843. She died in 1887 spending her married life in Mellor a
2 CONC nd later Blackburn. The Baileys had 7 children. Margaret married Ralph Eastham of Great Mitton (1822-1868) in all Hallows on 13 November 1848, spending her married life in the Wuerdle and later the Littleborough area of Lancashire. Margaret died&lt
2 CONC ;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;in 1887. The Easthams had 8 children.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;span lang="EN-GB"&gt;V) New Zealand&lt;/span&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&
2 CONC lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;As far as the sons of Isaac are concerned, the Sutcliffe line was continued by at least two of the males, William and James, to whom I will return later in this summary
2 CONC . Records of the Australian&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;immigrant board show that Robert Sutcliffe of Pendleton , son of Isaac and Alice, arrived in New South Wales aboard the vessel St. Vincent on 13 March 1849. He had taken advantage of&lt
2 CONC ;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;the available &amp;ldquo;assisted passage&amp;rdquo; scheme, which was offered at that time to workers such as blacksmiths, so that his voyage &amp;lsquo;only&amp;rsquo; cost him five pounds. The record states tha
2 CONC t he enjoyed good bodily health and required no medical assistance during the long voyage. Between 1855 and 1873 he can be found on electoral rolls and in Wise&amp;rsquo;s Trade Directory as an inhabitant of Wellington, New Zealand, residing in Dic
2 CONC kson Street and Willis Street. So far no records have been discovered which show marriage, the birth of children or even his death there.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;spa
2 CONC n lang="EN-GB"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;</line><line>&lt;span lang="EN-GB"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;</line><line>&lt;span lang="EN-GB"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;</line><line>&lt;span lang="EN-GB"&gt;VI) Accrington&lt;/span&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&g
2 CONC t;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;William moved to Accrington, where he married Mary Hacking in St James, Accrington on 8 September 18
2 CONC 49. His brother, James, followed suit in 1851 by marrying Hannah Burton on 22 March in Newchurch in Rossendale. At this time, according to the census of 1851, his occupation&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;was as a wheelwright and his wife wa
2 CONC s a woollen power loom weaver. Their son, James Isaac, was born on 2 September 1851 and baptised in Newchurch on 5 October 1851. William&amp;rsquo;s and Mary&amp;rsquo;s only son, Isaac, was born on&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;9 July 1850 a
2 CONC t Plantation Mill Street, Accrington. The 1851 census shows this address to be the domicile of Mary Hacking&amp;rsquo;s family and that William was now a labourer at the print works. By 1857 William had&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;slightly c
2 CONC hanged&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;the terms of his occupation,&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;still a labourer but at the brewery in Burnley Road, Accrington. 1857 was a tragic year for both William and his brother James being the yea
2 CONC r in which both their wives died. William married again on 4 September 1858 to Betty Riley and the 1861 census reveals a family reunited with William and his new wife, Betty, their son, Isaac, and James and his son, James Isaac, all residing at 18
2 CONC 2 Burnley Road, Accrington. William was still by occupation a labourer and James a wheelwright.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;The length of William&amp;rsquo;s second marriage, however, was also fated to be shortened by th
2 CONC e death of his wife, Betty, on 14 July 1867.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;For the second time, only a brief time after his former wife&amp;rsquo;s death, William remarried. This was to Mary Ann Pilkington on 22 February 1868, so that he, toge
2 CONC ther with his new wife, Mary Ann, and his son, Isaac, are all to be found on the 1871 census living at 41 Maudsley Street, Old Accrington. William was still a labourer. The son was listed as being a cotton weaver.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line
2 CONC >&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;James did not immediately remarry but in the 1871 census was living with relatives of Betty Riley at 41 Marsden Street, Old Accrington. Still a wheelwright, his son, James Isaac, had become an apprentice grocer.&lt;/span&gt;&l
2 CONC t;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;On 27 October 1876&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;James married the widow of Thomas Barnes (1826-1870). Her maiden name had been Alice Holt. The 1881 census reveals that James Isaac had now left his fat
2 CONC her and that Alice Barnes had a son, John Barnes aged twenty, who was an ironmonger along with a daughter, Rachel aged 16, a teacher, and George aged 14, who was also an ironmonger. James was described as formerly being a wheelwright. They were liv
2 CONC ing in 29 Marsden Street, Accrington. In the 1881 census the only change is that they are now all ten years older.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;On 8 June 1884 James Sutcliffe died at 41 Marsden Street, Accrington at the a
2 CONC ge of 54 years. His son, James Isaac, was in attendance at the death. At that time the son&amp;rsquo;s residence was in 8 Melbourne Street, Accrington.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;Similarly to James Isaac, William&amp;rs
2 CONC quo;s son, Isaac, had also left the residence shared with his father shortly after the 1871 census had been taken. 1873 saw the year of the marriages of both cousins; Isaac marrying Hannah Caroline Howard in Southport on 1 March 1873, followed by J
2 CONC ames Isaac to Margaret Cressey on 31 July at Accrington, St James. By moving to Southport (North Meols), Isaac had followed the example of his uncle George Hacking (1831-1912), under whose family roof in Plantation Mill Street, Accrington, he had b
2 CONC riefly lived as an infant at the beginning of his father&amp;rsquo;s marriage to Mary Hacking.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;William Sutcliffe&amp;rsquo;s later life points to an increase in prosperity if not in happiness
2 CONC . In the 1870s William started up in business as a brewer and shopkeeper at premises in 16 Sidney Street, Accrington, as evidenced in Barret&amp;rsquo;s directory of 1878. In 1879, according to Slater&amp;rsquo;s directory, he moved to 2 Manor Stre
2 CONC et, where he was described as a &amp;ldquo;shopkeeper and dealer in groceries and sundries&amp;rdquo;. By the 1881 census his business was situated in 5 Sidney Street, Accrington where he registered his occupation as &amp;ldquo;grocer and brewer&am
2 CONC p;rdquo;. Brewing suggests some kind of knowledge which he had gained at the brewery in Burnley Road as a labourer in the 1850s and 1860s. One possibility, which cannot be proven, is that he had worked for the Bank Brewery in Burnley Road, which wa
2 CONC s operational at that particular time. His mental state, however, was in turmoil and, according to the later inquest into his death on 18 June 1883, severe depression led to an attempted suicide on 9 December 1882 where he tried to cut his own thro
2 CONC at. Unfortunately, his mental problems did not abate and on 16 June 1883 he was found dead in Sydney Street by one of his employees, John Entwistle. The inquest into his death found that he had&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;&amp;ldquo;hanged h
2 CONC imself by the neck , with a rope from a hook in the wall of his cellar whilst of unsound mind&amp;rdquo;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;His will &amp;ldquo;proved at Lancaster the second day of August 1883&amp;rdquo;&lt;sp
2 CONC an&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;states that at this time he was living at 6 Manor Street so that his place of business and abode were separate. He left his &amp;ldquo;real and personal estate&amp;rdquo;, valued at 564 pounds and 7.5 pence net, to hi
2 CONC s third wife, Mary Ann. There is no mention of his son, Isaac, who at that time was living in North Meols. The inscription on his gravestone in Burnley Road Cemetery, Accrington ( Grave number EB 428) reveals not only the ages and dates of death of
2 CONC &lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;himself and his three wives but also two daughters born to his first wife, Mary Hacking, who had both died as infants.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;
2 CONC /em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span&gt;In Memory of the late William Sutcliffe who died June 16&lt;sup&gt;th&lt;/sup&gt; 1883 in his 62&lt;sup&gt;nd&lt;/sup&gt; year. Also Mary Ann wife of the above, born May 29&lt;sup&gt;th&l
2 CONC t;/sup&gt; 1827, died April 19&lt;sup&gt;th&lt;/sup&gt; 1901. Mary, wife of William Sutcliffe of Accrington, who died August 30&lt;sup&gt;th&lt;/sup&gt; 1857 aged 30 years and 10 months, interred at St James Church. Also Alice, their daughter, wh
2 CONC o died March 1&lt;sup&gt;st&lt;/sup&gt; 1856 aged 2 years and 4 months, interred at St James Church. Also Elizabeth, their daughter, who died December 1&lt;sup&gt;st&lt;/sup&gt; 1858 aged 3 years and 4 months, interred at St James Church. Also Bett
2 CONC y, his wife, who died July 14&lt;sup&gt;th&lt;/sup&gt; 1867 in the 51&lt;sup&gt;st&lt;/sup&gt; year of her age.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;Th
2 CONC e number of William&amp;rsquo;s remaining relatives resident in Accrington was increased after his nephew&amp;rsquo;s (James Isaac) marriage to Margaret Cressey in 1873. Jane Hannah Sutcliffe was born in 1874, James Richard in 1876 and John Willia
2 CONC m in 1879. James Isaac Sutcliffe had a number of different occupations up until his death in 1819 which included shopman, joiner&amp;rsquo;s labourer and carpenter. Jane Hannah married James Howarth in 1896 at St John the Evangelist, Accrington. I
2 CONC n the 1901 census James Isaac and his son, James Richard, were at 26 Norfolk Street, Accrington, a residence they shared with Jane Hannah, her husband, James, and baby daughter, Margaret; whereas Margaret Sutcliffe, James Isaac&amp;rsquo;s wife, wa
2 CONC s staying with her mother and father, Richard and Jane Cressey, at 6 Burnley Road, Accrington. John William Sutcliffe was also resident at this address.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;John William Sutcliffe married Grace Al
2 CONC ice Lees in 1900 and moved to Rawtenstall. At least two female children are known to have been born to this couple, Florence Gladys in 1901 and Winnie in 1909. For Jane Hannah and her husband, James Howarth, it is possible to locate three children
2 CONC . Margaret born 1900, Jane 1903 and Arthur 1907.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;James Richard Sutcliffe married Gertrude Nellie Townsend in 1902 and also had three children: Mollie born in 1903, Jim in 1905 and Margaret i
2 CONC n 1910. He remained in Accrington and with the onset of the first world war became one of that group known as the &amp;ldquo;Accrington Pals&amp;rdquo;, who joined up and saw action in Flanders. Tragically, James Richard never returned, being &amp;
2 CONC lsquo;killed in action&amp;rsquo; as a private in the 11&lt;sup&gt;th&lt;/sup&gt; Battalion of the East Lancashire Regiment on 6 August 1916.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;
2 CONC span lang="EN-GB"&gt;VII) Southport&lt;/span&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;Isaac Sutcliffe, born on 9 June 1850 in Plantation Mill Street, Accrington, had moved sometime afte
2 CONC r the 1871 census to join his uncle, George Hacking, in North Meols (Southport ). His occupation in the 1871 census is as a cotton weaver, but by the time of his marriage on 1 March 1873 to Hannah Caroline Howard&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;
2 CONC he has become a broker&amp;rsquo;s assistant to his uncle, George Hacking. Slater&amp;rsquo;s Directory for 1879 shows him to be self-reliant having taken over his uncle&amp;rsquo;s business. He is listed as a marine store dealer at 1 Back Virgini
2 CONC a Street. The 1881 census confirms and extends&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;this description by stating that he is a &amp;ldquo;marine store dealer employing two men and a coster woman&amp;rdquo;. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;
2 CONC &lt;span&gt;A &amp;ldquo;marine store dealer&amp;rdquo; was in modern terms a scrap/ scrap-metal/ merchant, at the outset possibly not far distant from the &amp;ldquo;rag and bone &amp;ldquo;man, who would call on all neighbourhoods and collect an
2 CONC y &amp;ldquo;scraps&amp;rdquo; he thought might be of further value. The &amp;ldquo;coster woman&amp;rdquo; was a woman who sold merchandise from a handcart. However small and insignificant the business seemed at this juncture, Isaac&amp;rsquo;s&lt
2 CONC ;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;legacy to his family after his death in 1908 would suggest a relatively high level of affluence.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;Isaac&amp;rsquo;s&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;busines
2 CONC s success, however, could not have been achieved without the support of his uncle, George Hacking, and it is therefore certainly necessary to refer to him in more detail before returning to Isaac.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;spa
2 CONC n&gt;George Hacking (1831-1912) was the brother of Isaac&amp;rsquo;s mother , Mary Hacking ( 1826-1857). George&amp;rsquo;s career is a further example of the self-made man; a late nineteenth century biography where financial independence and polit
2 CONC ical clout go hand in hand. George&amp;rsquo;s initial occupation, apprentice to an engraver, recorded on the 1851 census for Accrington, gives no indication as to&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;how his life and aspirations would change to suc
2 CONC h a radical extent. After his marriage to Elizabeth Holcroft in Clitheroe in 1854, he appeared on the 1861 census as a furniture broker. Sometime at the end of the decade, he moved to North Meols (Southport) where in the census of 1871 he had forme
2 CONC d the business which was later to be passed on to his nephew, Isaac Sutcliffe. The description of the business was very similar to that of&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;Isaac&amp;rsquo;s in 1881. &amp;ldquo; Furniture broker and marine store d
2 CONC ealer, employing two men and one woman&amp;rdquo;. Business must have been so profitable that he could take early retirement, so that by the census of 1881 he was referred to as a &amp;ldquo;retired broker&amp;rdquo;. In the meantime George had bee
2 CONC n elected Liberal councillor for Craven Ward, Southport. He continued this duty from 1874 until 1883 when he was elected as an alderman to the local council. In 1892 according to an article of the Liverpool Mercury ( Tuesday, 1 November) under &amp
2 CONC ;ldquo;New magistrates for Southport and Blackburn&amp;rdquo;, George Hacking of Ash Street was &amp;ldquo;placed on the commission of the peace for the borough of Southport&amp;rdquo;. Controversy surrounded George at the end of the century when h
2 CONC e no longer was &amp;lsquo;actively&amp;rsquo; resident at his house in 50 Ash street. By this time his influence and power had increased as he was not only a J.P. but also the chairman of the Southport Watch Committee. His move of permanent reside
2 CONC nce to his adopted daughter, Alice, and her husband in their house in Prescott in May 1899 meant that all rights to be considered a resident of Southport and therefore on the voting list for that place were necessarily contested. In the Liverpool M
2 CONC ercury of 22 September 1899, it was reported that although George had been resident in 50 Ash street for 14&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;years and a ratepayer in Southport for 34 years,&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;his present claim t
2 CONC o abode there was only token with no real substance. All the furniture had been taken out of the house and only &amp;ldquo; a bed and chair had been left to prove occupancy&amp;rdquo;. &amp;ldquo; He had not slept in the house since the middle of M
2 CONC ay&amp;rdquo;. This probably marks George&amp;rsquo;s retirement from civic and political life in Southport. Although dying in 1912, he was still able to outlive his nephew and attend Isaac&amp;rsquo;s funeral in 1908.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line>
2 CONC <line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;Isaac Sutcliffe and his family moved residence and business from Back Virginia Street to Tulketh Street after 1881.Together with his children, Elizabeth (1873-1965), William (1874-1951), Richard (1879-1955), Mary (1882-19
2 CONC 33) and Ann (1884-1970), and his wife, Hannah Caroline Howard (1849-1920), they all settled in at number 90 Tulketh Street; one of the few houses on this street which is still in existence today. Over the course of the next twenty years, Isaac wa
2 CONC s in possession of three properties in Tulketh Street. A glimpse into Seed&amp;rsquo;s Southport and District Directory 1924-1925 shows that this right of occupancy was still preserved after his death. Thus William Sutcliffe at 51 Tulketh Street, I
2 CONC saac Sutcliffe Marine Store Dealer at 53a and Richard Sutcliffe at 90 Tulketh Street were all registered as occupants in this street. Furthermore, Isaac&amp;rsquo;s daughter, Elizabeth, who married Edward Sutton (1859-1939) in 1901, was living wit
2 CONC h her husband at 88 Tulketh street.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;Naturally, Isaac&amp;rsquo;s business of &amp;lsquo;recycling the waste of others&amp;rsquo; was in sore need of&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;a depo
2 CONC t or storage yard, and the location of that in Tulketh Street did not meet with universal approbation, as verified by this article taken from the Southport Visiter of 23 September 1885 &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;n
2 CONC bsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span&gt;THE TULKETH STREET MARINE STORE &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br&gt; &lt;br&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;much has alread
2 CONC y been said and written regarding the marine store nuisance in Tulketh Street, yet on public grounds the serious nature of the case ought to be a sufficient reason for offering our extended comments. In the memorial sent to the Mayor, and read ou
2 CONC t at the last meeting of the Council, it was alleged that the business was not only offensive but prejudicial to health, and in other respects calculated to depreciate the value of property in the vicinity, on account of the premises being used fo
2 CONC r the purpose of storing various kinds of old rags, bones, fat and other refuse collected, which when stirred up for removal caused a sickening stench to permeate the atmosphere, to the annoyance alike of both residents and visitors.&lt;/span&gt;&l
2 CONC t;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br&gt; &lt;br&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;This was probably a time where the political influence of his uncle, George Hacking , a member of the Council, would have proven most e
2 CONC xpedient! Isaac did, however, find another location for his yard, albeit some years later. This was in Boundary Street at the old Tramshed Works , the former car shed and stables of the Birkdale and Southport Tramways Company. By 1897 the Town Coun
2 CONC cil had set up a Tramways Committee with a view to planning a comprehensive electric tramway system. After 12 December 1902 the old horse-drawn trams of the Birkdale and Southport Tramways Company had been completely faded out and replaced by the e
2 CONC lectric variety. At some point after this, Isaac bought or rented properties on Boundary street for his own business purposes. In 1956 these properties, numbers 97,99,101,103 and 103a Boundary street, still in the hands of the Sutcliffe family, wer
2 CONC e sold to Butterfield and Co. for 5,600 pounds. Today number 99a is the home of&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;Karwowski Motors and still known as the &amp;ldquo;Tramshed Works&amp;rdquo;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt
2 CONC ;Isaac died on 11 May 1908 at his home in 90 Tulketh Street. His obituary attested him &amp;ldquo;geniality and generosity&amp;rdquo; describing him as a &amp;ldquo;large-hearted man&amp;rdquo; whose &amp;ldquo;companionship was greatly appreciated
2 CONC &amp;rdquo;. Anecdotal evidence, passed down by family members throughout the years,&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;suggests an overindulgence in alcohol and a proclivity to give money to people he thought were suffering because of impoverishme
2 CONC nt - often on the way home after a stint in the local inn, which seems, in his case, to have been the Crown in Birkdale. His burial certainly brought out a large company of mourners, the Southport Visiter recording no less than twelve coaches, whic
2 CONC h followed his coffin to the&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;Cemetery. Present was a certain John Slinger of Pendleton, thus maintaining, almost 100 years after the marriage of Alice Slinger and Isaac Sutcliffe in 1817, the Southport Sutcliffe
2 CONC s contact to the village of Pendleton.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;The net value of his personal estate left to his wife Hannah Caroline (nee Howard) was valued at the considerable amount of 3,494 pounds and 5 pence. Han
2 CONC nah Caroline died on 26 January 1920. She was the daughter of Richard Howard (1801-1885) and his second wife, Elizabeth McKenzie of Scotland (1817-1879). Her obituary identified her prominently with the work of Christ Church, where she had long bee
2 CONC n a&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;member of the mother&amp;rsquo;s meeting. She had also taken a deep interest in the Christ Church Mission in Tulketh Street. The close contact to Pendleton can also be documented, even at this late juncture, b
2 CONC y the presence of John Slinger, the same mourner who had been in attendance twelve years earlier at the funeral of Isaac.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;Of the female children, only Elizabeth Sutcliffe was to marry. After h
2 CONC er marriage to Edward Sutton on 4 July 1901, she gave birth to three children. Edward Sutton had been married before to Jane Ann Wright in 1882, but the marriage was curtailed by Jane Ann&amp;rsquo;s death in 1884. He was at that time a close neigh
2 CONC bour of the Sutcliffes in Tulketh Street.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;Edward, who started his working career as a Cartwright,&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;joined the police force as a constable third class on 28 October 188
2 CONC 1 and quickly moved up the ranks, making sergeant on 11 February 1890 and inspector on 9 January 1902. He was superannuated on 13 August 1908.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;After their parents&amp;rsquo; deaths , the fou
2 CONC r children continued the business of &amp;lsquo;Isaac Sutcliffe Marine Store Dealer&amp;rsquo; as a partnership right up until the death of Richard Sutcliffe on 1 May 1955. The business was no longer so profitable but, nevertheless, had stood the p
2 CONC ressure of time and competition for almost&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;eighty years. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;The last surviving member of the Sutcliffe family remaining in Southport was Denis Richard Sutcliff
2 CONC e, son of Richard, born 13 May 1919 in Wallasey,&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;who died&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;on 20 February 1993 at Queenscourt Hospice, Southport. Denis had worked as an accountant for the Southport gas board, an
2 CONC d after his retirement been involved with the financial management of the Bold Hotel.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
